// `str_buf_append` takes the buffer it is handed.
//
// The call returns either the same buffer, when there was room, or a fresh one,
// when there was not. Only the runtime knows which, and the two cases have to be
// owned the same way, so the contract is that the call consumes its first
// argument: the old buffer is released inside the call, and the argument is dead
// afterwards.
//
// Neither of the two alternatives works, which is why it is the contract and not
// a choice. If the caller kept the old buffer, then `buf = str_buf_append(buf,
// s)` would release the destination's own value whenever the append grew in
// place, which is the common case. If the caller dropped it, nothing released it
// and the old buffer leaked on every append that reallocated.
//
// So the argument is marked moved at the call. That gives the scope teardown
// something to skip and turns a later read into a diagnostic rather than a use of
// released bytes. The flag is cleared by the assignment that receives the result,
// which is what lets the builder below append in a loop.
//
// This case checks the shapes. It cannot check that nothing leaks, because a
// golden test only sees output. The leak was 4285 bytes across 12 allocations for
// a loop appending one byte two thousand times, and `-fsanitize=address` is
// clean on the first loop below now. What is left is a closure cell, which has no
// owner at all by design, and the `StringBuilder` object, which has no destructor
// for its buffer. Both are known and both are written down where they belong.

int main() {
    // The ordinary idiom, repeated. Each call consumes `b` and the assignment
    // gives it a value again.
    var b = str_buf_new(8);
    b = str_buf_append(b, "one ");
    b = str_buf_append(b, "two ");
    b = str_buf_append(b, "three");
    Console.WriteLog(b);

    // A buffer with room, so the append returns the same pointer. The teardown
    // still releases it once, because the argument is consumed either way.
    var roomy = str_buf_new(64);
    roomy = str_buf_append(roomy, "small");
    Console.WriteLog(roomy);
    Console.WriteLog(len(roomy));

    // The result used as an argument rather than stored. Nothing names the new
    // buffer afterwards, so it is released with the statement.
    var c = str_buf_new(0);
    Console.WriteLog(len(str_buf_append(c, "grown")));

    // A literal argument, which has nothing to release.
    var l = str_buf_append("lit", "eral");
    l = str_buf_append(l, " and more");
    Console.WriteLog(l);

    // And the builder, which is what the standard library actually calls.
    var sb = new StringBuilder();
    sb.append("a");
    sb.appendInt(42);
    sb.append("b");
    Console.WriteLog(sb.toString());
    Console.WriteLog(sb.length());
    sb.clear();
    Console.WriteLog(sb.isEmpty());
    var taken = sb.take();
    Console.WriteLog(len(taken));

    return 0;
}